  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Org Information — Determine Physical Locations", "technique": "T1591.001", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 09:02:17 CEST, Suricata logged the start of a systematic HTTP path-enumeration burst with data.srcip=91.211.247.155 against the corporate web application data.dstip=10.108.57.177. Over ~7 minutes the source issued 35+ sequential GET requests to organisation-specific paths — /about, /locations, /offices, /office-locations, /headquarters, /facilities, /emea, /apac, /americas, /branches, /data-centers, /map, /directions — a curated wordlist that targets physical-location and facility disclosure rather than technical vulnerabilities. The requests rotated across four browser User-Agent strings to blend in, but the path ordering is alphabetical-by-category and not human-navigation-like.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET SCAN Aggressive Web Crawl' (sig_id 2009358) fired repeatedly with data.srcip=91.211.247.155 against data.dstip=10.108.57.177. The univariate trigger: single external source issuing >30 distinct GET requests to org-structure paths within a 10 minute window.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:02:17+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Org Information — Metadata Harvesting", "technique": "T1591.001", "what_happened": "At 09:11:05 CEST (after a ~60s inter-phase pause), the same data.srcip=91.211.247.155 requested site-metadata files: /sitemap.xml, /sitemap_index.xml, /robots.txt, /.well-known/security.txt, /page-sitemap.xml, /news-sitemap.xml. Metadata files leak the full URL inventory of the site in one request — an efficiency move that lets the attacker discover hidden location/office pages without brute-forcing every path. This is the classic crawl-optimisation step of an org-information scrape.", "chain_reason": "Continuation of the same Suricata 'ET SCAN Aggressive Web Crawl' (sig_id 2009358) correlation key data.srcip=91.211.247.155 -> data.dstip=10.108.57.177, with data.url values matching sitemap/robots metadata paths in a tight burst.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:11:05+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Org Information — Content Keyword Extraction", "technique": "T1591.001", "what_happened": "At 09:15:40 CEST (after a ~45s pause) the source re-fetched ONLY the HTTP-200 pages discovered in step 1 and parsed them for physical-address keywords (Street, Avenue, Suite, Floor, Building, Tower, Cedex) and country names (France, Germany, UK, USA, Singapore...). The selective re-fetch of confirmed-live pages — instead of re-crawling the whole wordlist — is a high-operator-skill marker: the attacker is mining content, not enumerating paths.", "chain_reason": "Repeated Suricata web-crawl alerts with data.srcip=91.211.247.155 -> data.dstip=10.108.57.177 where data.url is the subset of paths that returned HTTP 200 in step 1, within the 09:15-09:21 window.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:15:40+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Org Information — Contact Form Office Enumeration", "technique": "T1591.001", "what_happened": "At 09:20:55 CEST (after a ~30s pause) the source switched from GET to HTTP POST against /contact, submitting 12 forged region parameters (EMEA, APAC, Americas, Middle-East, LATAM, Africa, UK, France, Germany...) with a probe payload (name=J.+Martin, email=j.martin@external-corp.net). The intent is to trigger backend validation errors that leak the real list of valid offices. The POST-to-/contact burst from the same source is the final org-enumeration step and may also trip the WAF.", "chain_reason": "Suricata observed data.http.method=POST with data.url=/contact from data.srcip=91.211.247.155 -> data.dstip=10.108.57.177, 12 requests in ~4 minutes, immediately following the GET-based crawl phases — same correlation key, method change.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:20:55+0200"}
